msgid "<0/> has no ending, and it serves both he or she and they. That makes <1/> irregular: other verbs add an ending there too, as lesson {regularVerbs} shows."
msgstr "<0/> has no ending, and it serves both he or she and they. That makes <1/> irregular: other verbs add an ending there too, as lesson {regularVerbs} shows."

msgid "<0/> is he and she alike; Estonian pronouns have no gender. <1/> is also the polite you to one person, like French vous."
msgstr "<0/> is he and she alike; Estonian pronouns have no gender. <1/> is also the polite you to one person, like French vous."

msgid "<0/> means to be. Its ending changes with the person: <1/> is I am, <2/> is you are. Tap an ending."
msgstr "<0/> means to be. Its ending changes with the person: <1/> is I am, <2/> is you are. Tap an ending."

msgid "<0>On</0> is both he or she is and they are. Only the pronoun tells them apart."
msgstr "<0>On</0> is both he or she is and they are. Only the pronoun tells them apart."

msgid "<0>Ta</0> is both he and she. <1>Te</1> is you, to more than one, and polite you to one person."
msgstr "<0>Ta</0> is both he and she. <1>Te</1> is you, to more than one, and polite you to one person."

msgid "10 minutes"
msgstr "10 minutes"

msgid "2 quizzes"
msgstr "2 quizzes"

msgid "All of them. You can hear who it’s about."
msgstr "All of them. You can hear who it’s about."

msgid "All six. Every ending was right."
msgstr "All six. Every ending was right."

msgid "Answers"
msgstr "Answers"

msgid "Because the ending already says who, the pronoun can go. Both lines below mean I’m Mari. A sentence can drop the pronoun, but not the verb."
msgstr "Because the ending already says who, the pronoun can go. Both lines below mean I’m Mari. A sentence can drop the pronoun, but not the verb."

msgid "Each pronoun has two forms. Say the short one, <0>ma</0>, and keep the long one, <1>mina</1>, for stress."
msgstr "Each pronoun has two forms. Say the short one, <0>ma</0>, and keep the long one, <1>mina</1>, for stress."

msgid "Endings of olema"
msgstr "Endings of olema"

msgid "Estonian has six words for people, and each comes in a long and a short form that mean the same. <0/> and <1/> are both I. The short one, in colour below, is the one you’ll hear most. Tap any word."
msgstr "Estonian has six words for people, and each comes in a long and a short form that mean the same. <0/> and <1/> are both I. The short one, in colour below, is the one you’ll hear most. Tap any word."

msgid "Fill the ending"
msgstr "Fill the ending"

msgctxt "pronoun form"
msgid "Form"
msgstr "Form"

msgid "Go back to the endings above, then try again."
msgstr "Go back to the endings above, then try again."

msgid "he or she"
msgstr "he or she"

msgid "he or she is"
msgstr "he or she is"

msgid "He or she is a teacher."
msgstr "He or she is a teacher."

msgid "He or she is tired."
msgstr "He or she is tired."

msgid "he or she is; they are"
msgstr "he or she is; they are"

msgid "Hear a sentence and pick who it’s about. Some drop the pronoun, so listen to the end of the verb. With <0/>, only the pronoun tells <1/> from <2/>."
msgstr "Hear a sentence and pick who it’s about. Some drop the pronoun, so listen to the end of the verb. With <0/>, only the pronoun tells <1/> from <2/>."

